{"file":{"content":"The last arrow C2 is now the only genuinely new theorem left:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{phase-only shadow bias}\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{residue-edge trap}\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{coherent lift attempt}\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{coboundary or descent}.\n}\n]\n\nI do **not** think it follows formally from the finite spectral gap. It is the deterministic/quenched wall. But it is now sharp enough that we can formulate a proof strategy that is finite, checkable, and capable of being falsified.\n\nThe best next attack is not another annealed Fourier estimate. It is a **max-plus / potential-certificate theorem** for biased residue-edge traps.\n\n## 1. What C2 must prove\n\nLet\n\n[\nT(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2^{b(x)}},\n\\qquad b(x)=v_2(3x+1),\n]\n\nand for a non-coboundary character (\\chi') put\n\n[\nz=\\chi'(2),\n\\qquad\nF_{\\chi'}(x_n,x_{n+1})=z^{-b_n}.\n]\n\nLet\n\n[\nG_\\chi=\\mathbb E_{\\rm geom}[z^{-b}]\n=\\sum_{b\\ge1}2^{-b}z^{-b}.\n]\n\nA phase-only bad window is one where, after choosing a phase (\\omega\\in S^1),\n\n[\n\\frac1M\\sum_{n=N}^{N+M-1}\n\\Re\\left(\\omega(z^{-b_n}-G_\\chi)\\right)\\ge\\varepsilon,\n]\n\nbut\n\n[\n\\frac1M\\sum b_n\n\\approx \\log_2 3,\n]\n\nso C1 does not force descent.\n\nThe C2 theorem should say:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{such a window cannot persist indefinitely without either descent or an exact coboundary trap.}\n}\n]\n\nSince (\\chi') is non-coboundary, the coboundary trap is excluded by your classification theorem.\n\n## 2. The right finite object: biased residue-edge trap\n\nFor fixed (r), let (G_r) be the residue transition graph with vertices\n\n[\nU_{3^r}\n]\n\nand directed edges\n\n[\na\\xrightarrow{b}S_b(a):=(3a+1)2^{-b}\\pmod{3^r}.\n]\n\nEach edge carries two labels:\n\n[\ng(b)=\\log_2 3-b\n]\n\nand\n\n[\n\\phi_\\chi(b)=\\Re\\left(\\omega(z^{-b}-G_\\chi)\\right).\n]\n\nHere (g) is logarithmic growth, and (\\phi_\\chi) is the real non-coboundary shadow observable.\n\nA phase-only biased orbit segment gives an empirical edge measure (\\mu_I) on (G_r) with\n\n[\n\\int \\phi_\\chi,d\\mu_I\\ge\\varepsilon,\n]\n\nwhile the no-descent / near-critical condition says roughly\n\n[\n\\int g,d\\mu_I\\ge -o(1).\n]\n\nSo C2 becomes:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{no coherently liftable edge measure can have both }\n\t\\int \\phi_\\chi\\ge\\varepsilon\n\t\\text{ and }\n\t\\int g\\ge0,\n\t\\text{ unless it is coboundary.}\n}\n]\n\nThis is now a finite optimization statement, except for the phrase “coherently liftable.”\n\nThat phrase is the arithmetic content.\n\n## 3. The max-plus certificate formulation\n\nThis is the most useful way to attempt a proof.\n\nFor a finite trap (H\\subseteq G_r), try to find:\n\n* a potential\n[\n\\Psi:U_{3^r}\\to\\mathbb R,\n]\n* a multiplier\n[\n\\lambda\\ge0,\n]\n* a margin\n[\n\\delta>0,\n]\n\nsuch that for every edge (e:a\\xrightarrow{b}a') in the trap,\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tg(b)+\\lambda(\\phi_\\chi(b)-\\varepsilon)\n\t+\\Psi(a')-\\Psi(a)\n\t\\le -\\delta.\n}\n]\n\nThen summing along a window gives\n\n[\n\\sum g(b_n)\n+\n\\lambda\\sum(\\phi_\\chi(b_n)-\\varepsilon)\n+\n\\Psi(a_{N+M})-\\Psi(a_N)\n\\le -\\delta M.\n]\n\nIf the window has\n\n[\n\\frac1M\\sum\\phi_\\chi(b_n)\\ge\\varepsilon,\n]\n\nthen\n\n[\n\\sum g(b_n)\n\\le\n-\\delta M+O_\\Psi(1).\n]\n\nBut\n\n[\n\\sum g(b_n)\n===========\n\nM\\log_2 3-\\sum b_n\n]\n\nis exactly the logarithmic drift term. Therefore for long enough (M),\n\n[\n\\log_2 x_{N+M}<\\log_2 x_N\n]\n\nup to the small positive correction\n\n[\n\\sum \\log_2\\left(1+\\frac1{3x_n}\\right).\n]\n\nSo descent follows.\n\nThus C2 would follow from the existence of this finite potential certificate for every non-coboundary phase-only trap.\n\nThis is a concrete theorem target.\n\n## 4. Dual form: max cycle mean\n\nBy max-plus duality, the potential certificate exists iff every directed cycle (\\Gamma) in the trap satisfies\n\n[\n\\frac1{|\\Gamma|}\n\\sum_{e\\in\\Gamma}\n\\left[\ng(e)+\\lambda(\\phi_\\chi(e)-\\varepsilon)\n\\right]\n<0.\n]\n\nEquivalently, define the max-plus pressure\n\n[\nP_H(\\lambda)\n============\n\n\\max_{\\Gamma\\subset H}\n\\frac1{|\\Gamma|}\n\\sum_{e\\in\\Gamma}\n\\left[g(e)+\\lambda\\phi_\\chi(e)\\right].\n]\n\nThen the certificate exists if\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tP_H(\\lambda)<\\lambda\\varepsilon.\n}\n]\n\nThis is a finite, checkable condition.\n\nSo the C2 proof can be split into two parts:\n\n[\n\\text{phase-only bias}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{trap }H;\n]\n\n[\n\\text{coherent liftability of }H\n\\Rightarrow\n\\exists\\lambda:\\ P_H(\\lambda)<\\lambda\\varepsilon\n\\quad\\text{unless }H\\text{ is coboundary}.\n]\n\nThe second line is the deterministic rigidity theorem.\n\n## 5. Why this is not automatic\n\nIf (H) is the full residue graph (G_r), the certificate may fail. The full finite graph can contain spurious cycles that do not correspond to positive integer orbits. For example, low-(b) cycles modulo (3^r) may have positive max-plus growth even though they do not lift coherently to actual positive orbits.\n\nTherefore the certificate must be applied to **coherently liftable traps**, not arbitrary finite subgraphs.\n\nThis is the essential arithmetic issue.\n\nThe final missing theorem should be phrased as:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\textbf{Coherent-lift max-plus rigidity.}\n}\n]\n\nAny finite residue-edge trap that lifts coherently through all moduli (3^R), has non-coboundary shadow bias, and avoids descent admits a max-plus cycle obstruction. That obstruction must be an exact coboundary, hence impossible for non-coboundary (\\chi').\n\nThis is the right “last arrow.”\n\n## 6. How to define coherent lift attempt\n\nGiven a trap (H_r\\subset G_r), define its lift to level (R>r) as the set of edges\n\n[\nH_R\\subset G_R\n]\n\nwhose projection to (G_r) lies in (H_r) and which occur with positive empirical frequency in the orbit window.\n\nA coherent lift attempt is a projective family\n\n[\nH_r\\leftarrow H_{r+1}\\leftarrow H_{r+2}\\leftarrow\\cdots\n]\n\nwith:\n\n1. nonvanishing edge mass at every level;\n2. phase bias bounded below:\n[\n\\int\\phi_\\chi,d\\mu_R\\ge\\varepsilon;\n]\n3. near-critical drift:\n[\n\\int g,d\\mu_R\\ge -o(1);\n]\n4. no descent on the corresponding orbit segment.\n\nThen C2 becomes:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{No non-coboundary coherent lift attempt exists without descent.}\n}\n]\n\nThis is still hard, but it is now precise.\n\n## 7. The proof attempt that could work\n\nThe proof should try to show:\n\n[\n\\text{coherent lift attempt}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{periodic max-plus obstruction}.\n]\n\nSince each (H_R) is finite, any near-maximal no-descent segment contains a near-critical cycle in (H_R). More explicitly, by cycle decomposition of finite directed flows, the empirical edge measure decomposes into directed cycles:\n\n[\n\\mu_R=\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\mu_{\\Gamma_j}+O(\\text{boundary}/M).\n]\n\nIf\n\n[\n\\int g,d\\mu_R\\ge-o(1),\n\\qquad\n\\int \\phi_\\chi,d\\mu_R\\ge\\varepsilon,\n]\n\nthen at least one cycle (\\Gamma_j) satisfies a comparable combined inequality.\n\nSo the trap produces finite cycles with:\n\n[\n\\overline g(\\Gamma_j)\\ge -o(1),\n]\n\nand\n\n[\n\\overline \\phi_\\chi(\\Gamma_j)\\ge\\varepsilon/2.\n]\n\nNow lift those cycles as (R\\to\\infty). If the cycles remain coherent, they should converge to a (3)-adic periodic or eventually periodic object. The earlier periodic-word theorem says any nontrivial positive periodic object casts a non-coboundary shadow, but that alone does not contradict. You need the extra growth/descent condition.\n\nThe desired rigidity statement is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{a projectively coherent sequence of near-critical biased cycles either}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{becomes an actual integer cycle/coboundary, or forces negative drift at some lift.}\n}\n]\n\nThis is the finite-cycle version of C2 and may be more tractable than arbitrary orbit segments.\n\n## 8. Concrete computational program\n\nRun the max-plus certificate search. This is the right next computation.\n\nFor fixed (r,\\chi'), and a grid of (\\varepsilon):\n\n1. Enumerate candidate traps (H) arising from high-deviation windows.\n2. For each trap, compute\n[\nP_H(\\lambda)\n============\n\n\\max_{\\Gamma}\n\\overline{g+\\lambda\\phi_\\chi}(\\Gamma)\n]\nover (\\lambda\\ge0).\n3. Check whether\n[\n\\inf_{\\lambda\\ge0}\\left(P_H(\\lambda)-\\lambda\\varepsilon\\right)<0.\n]\n4. If yes, extract the potential (\\Psi) solving\n[\ng+\\lambda(\\phi-\\varepsilon)+\\Delta\\Psi\\le-\\delta.\n]\n5. Test whether the certificate remains valid under one or more lifts (r\\to r+1).\n\nFor observed phase-only high-deviation windows, this will tell whether C2 is likely true by finite potential certificates.\n\nThe decisive table should be:\n\n[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}\n\tr & \\chi' & \\varepsilon & \\min_\\lambda(P_H(\\lambda)-\\lambda\\varepsilon) & \\delta\\\n\t\\hline\n\\end{array}\n]\n\nIf every observed trap has a certificate, the C2 proof has a concrete path.\n\n## 9. A theorem you may be able to prove first\n\nStart with a **finite-window certified descent theorem**:\n\nFor fixed (r,\\chi',\\varepsilon), suppose an orbit window has shadow bias (\\ge\\varepsilon), and its empirical support (H\\subset G_r) admits a max-plus certificate:\n\n[\ng+\\lambda(\\phi-\\varepsilon)+\\Delta\\Psi\\le-\\delta.\n]\n\nThen the orbit descends within the window once\n\n[\nM>\n\\frac{\\operatorname{osc}\\Psi+O(M/x_N)}{\\delta}.\n]\n\nThis theorem is immediate and should be written down. It gives the exact certificate-to-descent mechanism.\n\nThen the only open part is:\n\n[\n\\text{every coherent non-coboundary trap admits such a certificate.}\n]\n\nThis separates the deterministic part cleanly.\n\n## 10. What about the finite spectral gap?\n\nThe finite spectral gap should be used as evidence and as an annealed counterpart, but not as the proof of C2.\n\nHowever, it can guide the certificate search. The annealed gap says non-coboundary bias cannot be stationary under the geometric branch weights. The max-plus certificate asks for the deterministic analogue:\n\n[\n\\text{non-coboundary bias cannot be supported by any no-descent coherent path.}\n]\n\nThese are parallel but distinct.\n\n## 11. Piece A and B should stay in the paper\n\nThe periodic-word theorem is good and should remain.\n\nThe elementary inverse proposition is also good:\n\n[\n|D_N(\\chi')|\\ge\\varepsilon\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{letter bias}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{residue-edge trap}.\n]\n\nBut now add the max-plus certificate theorem after it:\n\n[\n\\text{residue-edge trap + max-plus certificate}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{descent}.\n]\n\nThis makes the route operational.\n\n## 12. The last arrow in one display\n\nI would revise the final route display to:\n\n[\n\\text{phase-only shadow bias}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{residue-edge trap }H\n\\Rightarrow\n\\begin{cases}\n\t\\text{max-plus descent certificate},\\\n\t\\text{or coherent lift obstruction}.\n\\end{cases}\n]\n\nThen:\n\n[\n\\text{certificate}\\Rightarrow\\text{descent}.\n]\n\nAnd the remaining open theorem is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{every coherent non-coboundary lift trap admits a certificate.}\n}\n]\n\nThis is more precise than “coherent lift attempt (\\Rightarrow) coboundary or descent.”\n\n## 13. What could fail?\n\nThere are two possible failure modes.\n\n### Failure mode 1: spurious finite traps\n\nAt small (r), there may be traps with no certificate, but they do not lift coherently. That is harmless. It means the theorem must be projective in (r), not fixed-level.\n\n### Failure mode 2: genuine coherent trap without certificate\n\nThis would be a serious obstruction: a (3)-adic deterministic object with phase bias and no negative drift. It might correspond to a non-positive (3)-adic cycle or a divergent symbolic orbit. If found, it would either refute the route or identify a new object needing elimination.\n\nThe certificate search will distinguish these.\n\n## 14. Bottom line\n\nYou cannot prove C2 directly from the current finite operator theorem. The next proof should be built around a deterministic max-plus certificate.\n\nThe immediate theorem to add is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{shadow-biased trap + finite max-plus certificate}\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{descent}.\n}\n]\n\nThen the remaining hard theorem becomes:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{every coherently liftable non-coboundary trap has such a certificate.}\n}\n]\n\nThis is the smallest rigorous form of the final deterministic rigidity problem.\n\nSo the next concrete work is:\n\n1. define (H), (\\phi_\\chi), and (g);\n2. prove the certificate-to-descent lemma;\n3. run max-plus certificate searches on observed phase-only traps;\n4. study which uncertified traps fail to lift coherently;\n5. formulate/prove coherent-lift max-plus rigidity.\n\nThat is the most plausible route to C2.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_31.md","numLines":553,"startLine":1,"totalLines":553},"type":"text"}